    sum: "Worn tilted on the head with the dancer's face hidden behind cloth, Makonde lipiko helmet masks animate jando initiation and village celebrations with scarified ancestor beauty.",
    desc: "Among the Makonde of the Ruvuma frontier, the mapiko masquerade centres on the lipiko, a carved helmet mask worn on top of the head and tipped back so the performer's real face is veiled beneath a long cloth costume — the mask's own face becomes the character. Female-mask lipiko show idealised features, elaborate braided coiffures and fine cicatrization marks; companions in the set include grotesques and animal characters, and the masked dancer's surprise entrances discipline and delight jando initiates. Carvers work hardwood with chisel and scorched-black patina, and masterpieces from the early colonial decades entered the Met, the Fowler and Berlin collections, where the Masks (Lipiko) type remains a touchstone of East African sculpture. Mapiko troupes still perform at initiations, funerals and independence-era festivals in Mozambique and Tanzania.",
