    desc: "The akua'ba is an Akan carved wooden figure with a large flattened disc head, ringed neck and tapered cylindrical body — a formal reduction so clean it became a modernist favourite. Women seeking pregnancy commissioned, carried, bathed and be-jewelled the figure as a surrogate child, and after a birth it was dedicated at a shrine; the founding legend tells of the barren Akua, mocked for her devotion, whose 'child' (akua'ba) became the archetype. Asante types favour the tall disc head and Akan ideal features, while Fante variants grow crests and multiple heads; carvers in blackened wood produced them alongside court regalia. Met and British Museum collections hold canonical examples, and the silhouette persists on Ghanaian textiles, logo art and tourist carvings.",
