    sum: "Tall carved staffs topped with gilded proverb finials, held by Akan court linguists (okyeame) so that every speech at a chieftaincy durbar is a sculptural riddle.",
    desc: "In Akan courts the okyeame — the linguist who speaks for the chief — carries the okyeame poma, a slender staff whose gilded finial compresses a proverb into sculpture: a hen pecking corn (the proverb on chick discipline), a bird looking backward, chameleons, or the tongue-and-teeth pair warning that words cut. Finials are carved wood sheathed in gold leaf or cast in brass, rising over cloth-wrapped shafts, and new designs constantly add contemporary commentary to the classical repertory. During adowa drumming at durbars, funerals and Odwira festivals, linguists weave the staff's meanings into oratory so the object becomes an argument. Met, British Museum and Ghanaian palace collections document the type from the nineteenth century, and the form remains the most distinctive genre of Akan miniature sculpture.",
