    sum: "Bold appliquéd and painted banners (frankaa) of the Fante asafo military companies, staging proverbs, lions and artillery in graphic scenes waved at festivals and funerals.",
    desc: "Every Fante coastal town is divided into numbered asafo companies — military-ceremonial associations — whose frankaa flags announce them in cloth theatre: a lion trampling a prostrate foe, a hand catching a knife, soldiers and cannons stitched beside crowns and Union Jacks, each a proverb about who dares what. Flags are made by reverse appliqué and machine stitching in ferocious colour, or painted on imported cotton, and sewn by specialist families of flagmakers. At festivals and posu funerals, flagmen dance the frankaa in mock combat across company boundaries, darting and whirling to drums while rivals score each other's imagery, and new flags continue to add airplanes, mobile phones and politicians to the lexicon. Museums from the Fowler to the British Museum treat the genre as West Africa's great graphic-appliqué art.",
