    p: "19th century–present", era: "Zulu domestic and ritual vessel tradition",
    sum: "Globular black Zulu beer vessels coil-built by women, burnished to graphite sheen and bossed with amasumpa studs, served to ancestors and guests at every marriage and feast.",
    desc: "The ukhamba is the Zulu beer-drinking vessel: a wide-bellied, narrow-necked pot coil-built from river clay by women potters, fired open-air and burnished with a stone to a black graphite glow before decoration with raised amasumpa studs and incised grooves in bands around the shoulder. It is built for utshwala, the sorghum beer drunk communally through wide mouths, and its social role is ritual as much as domestic — beer for the amadlozi ancestors, wedding-present sets, and graded sizes for rank. The dynasty of Nesta Nala and her daughters carried ukhamba aesthetics into the international studio-craft market, with galleries from Pretoria to New York exhibiting the pots as sculpture. Collectors prize the tight geometry of the neck, the smooth black burnish and the stud-banded relief typical of Nongoma-area work.",
    col: [{ h: "#171514", n: "graphite burnish black" }, { h: "#4d4a45", n: "fired clay grey" }, { h: "#8a5a33", n: "beer ochre" }, { h: "#c9b891", n: "sorghum straw" }, { h: "#2f4f3a", n: "kraal grass green" }],
