    sum: "The Bozo master boatbuilders of the Niger inland delta sew great cargo pirogues plank to plank without frames, floating the entire river economy of Mopti on their hulls.",
    desc: "The Bozo — 'masters of the water' in their own telling — are the fisher-boatbuilder people of Mali's Niger inland delta, and their pirogues are the delta's circulatory system: long dugout and plank-hulled canoes, and the giant motorised pinasses that ferry grain, livestock and pilgrims between Mopti, Djenné and Timbuktu. Bozo shipwrights join African mahogany planks edge to edge, pegged and sewn, caulk the seams with fibre, and launch hulls that carry tonnes under patched square sails; the Mopti wharf at low water is a forest of their keels and a bazaar of their fish. River cosmology surrounds the trade — offerings before first launch, griot songs for the floods — and the annual high-water season turns the port into Mali's great living image. Ethnographic films and festival races have made the painted pirogue a national emblem.",
