    desc: "The Kotoko, descendants of the ancient Sao city-states of the Logone-Chari rivers, built in ferey — sun-dried mud brick moulded by hand — and raised at Logone-Birni a royal palace whose entrance ramp, throne courts and women's quarters were sheathed in buttressed, decorated brickwork. Relief work runs in chevrons, zigzag water-channels and raised prismatic courses that drain rain while dignifying the wall, a functional ornament parallel to the famous Musgum huts downstream. French ethnologists Jean-Paul and Anne Lebeuf documented and excavated the palace in the 1930s, and the reconstructed palace gate remains one of Central Africa's signature historic monuments. Kotoko masons still repair the ramparts seasonally, keeping alive a building culture where the wall is carved as if from earth itself.",
