    p: "16th century–present", era: "walled Islamic city culture",
    sum: "Inside Harar's walled Jugol, the traditional Harari house (ge gar) arranges platform seating, hanging baskets and wall niches crammed with treasured objects into one of Africa's great interior traditions.",
    desc: "Harar Jugol — the fourth holy city of Islam, UNESCO-listed with its five gates and 80-odd mosques — preserves the ge gar, the Harari house type whose single principal room is built as a total interior artwork: tiered daybed platforms ring the walls, a central fire pit sits beneath a hanging reed-work canopy, and the walls are pierced with arrays of red-edged niches where generations stack Chinese porcelain, rifles, baskets, clocks and trophies — display as genealogy. Coil-woven baskets hang in tiers from the ceiling, women's domestic ritual governs the space, and red, green and white pigment schemes mark household wealth. African, Islamic and Indian Ocean currents meet in the room's geometry, which travelers from Rimbaud's century to today record as Harar's living heart.",
