    sum: "The Société des Ambianceurs et des Personnes Élégantes — Congolese dandies in head-to-toe designer tailoring who stage elegance itself as discipline, contest and escape in Brazzaville and Kinshasa.",
    desc: "La Sape grew from the colonial-era envy of French tailoring in Brazzaville's Bacongo district into a structured brotherhood — the Société des Ambianceurs et des Personnes Élégantes — whose sapeurs own immaculate suits by named designers, know colour codes and ritual insults of the trade, and parade through mud-walled streets with umbrellas and rolled trousers as theatre. Papa Wemba crowned it a religion of cloth ('the kitendi religion'), Kinshasa added its own flamboyance, and the Paris and Brussels diaspora turned sape into remittance glamour: months of wages on a jacket, worn once. Films, the 2014 Guinness commercial and street photography (Hector Mediavilla's long projects) fixed the image — dignity assembled against failed states — while neighbourhood 'ambiance' contests keep scoring cut, colour and shoe polish weekly.",
