    sum: "Cape Verde's melancholic, fado-adjacent song genre in Kriolu — the music of sodade and voluntary exile — carried to the world by Cesária Évora and UNESCO-listed in 2019.",
    desc: "Morna is the Barlavento islands' slow, minor-key song form: a solo voice over cavaquinho, violão, violin and clarinet, phrasing long vowels of Kriolu in themes of sodade — the ache of absence and migration that structures Cape Verdean life. Nineteenth-century port culture braided Portuguese fado and modinha with African and Brazilian currents, and the poet Eugénio Tavares on Brava fixed the genre's lyric seriousness. Cesária Évora, the 'Barefoot Diva' of Mindelo, carried morna through world stages in the 1990s, her cigarettes-and-shawls gravity becoming the genre's global face. In 2019 UNESCO inscribed 'Morna, musical practice of Cape Verde' on the Intangible Heritage list, and in Mindelo's cafés the form is still taught instrument by instrument, island by island.",
