    cat: "Music & Sonic Culture", c: "Creole drum-and-voice tradition", e: "regional_tradition", o: "Réunion (Creole community)", g: "Réunion island, Creole diaspora",
    p: "19th century–present", era: "post-slavery ancestor service",
    sum: "The hypnotic drum-and-voice music of Réunion's Creole descendants of enslaved Malagasy and Africans — driven by rouler drum and kayamb rattle, long suppressed, now UNESCO-listed.",
    desc: "Maloya grew from the ancestor-service gatherings of Réunion's enslaved and engagé workers: sung in Creole, danced in circling steps around the rouler (a rum-barrel bass drum), the pikèr stick and the kayamb — a slatted reed board of seeds shaken in short, insistent strokes. Its call-and-response commemorates ancestral dead and plantation labour, and for a century the colonial state and the Church banned or mocked it, binding the music to clandestine yards and to the island's Communist and pro-independence movements. Singer-instrument maker Danyèl Waro led the revival from the 1970s, refusing to let the form become folklore, and UNESCO inscribed maloya on the Intangible Heritage list in 2009 — the first French nomination of that kind. Today maloya fuses appear in island rock while the kabaré service form continues in family yards.",
    col: [{ h: "#7a2e1d", n: "rum-barrel drum red" }, { h: "#c8b18a", n: "kayamb reed tan" }, { h: "#2b2b28", n: "yard-night black" }, { h: "#5c7a52", n: "cane-leaf green" }, { h: "#d9c7a7", n: "volcan-ash buff" }],
