    sum: "Mauritius' exuberant Creole dance-song — ravanne frame drum, maravanne shaker and improvised couplets over swaying hips — born on the plantation and UNESCO-listed as sega tipik.",
    desc: "Sega arose among enslaved Malagasy and African workers on Mauritius and its satellites: at night gatherings around fires, the ravanne frame drum and maravanne rattle drove rolling rhythms beneath Creole couplets — often improvised, teasing and mournful by turns — while dancers swayed with flat feet and rolling hips in long gathered skirts. Its greatest figure, Ti Frère (Alphonse Ravaton), codified the classic repertory in the 20th century, and songs like his 'Ravine Blanche' kept the form within village memory. Tourist beaches flattened sega into showbiz, but the traditional core — sega tipik — was inscribed by UNESCO in 2014 as the musical art of the Creole community, and Rodrigues and Chagossian sega variants carry the genre across the archipelago with their own accents.",
