    desc: "Famadihana, the highland Malagasy ritual centred on Merina and Betsileo country, gathers extended families every five to seven years to open the family tomb, lift out the wrapped dead, and celebrate: fresh lamba mena silk shrouds are wrapped in careful folds, bodies are carried dancing around the tomb to hira gasy bands, and guests are fed while elders speak to the razana as living kin. The ritual expresses the highland belief that the razana remain present members of the lineage, needing periodic honour, warmth and fresh wrapping; tombs (fasana) are the real houses of the family and their upkeep outranks that of the living. Blending church funerals with ancestral religion, famadihana has been criticised on health grounds yet persists as the highlands' greatest social event — dates set by astrologers in the dry austral winter.",
