    desc: "Ijele is the monumental masquerade of the Anambra Igbo: a bamboo-and-cloth tower some five metres tall, tiered like a moving shrine, its surfaces crowded with figurines, mirrors, cloth panels and carved symbols, crowned by an umbrella canopy and attended by its own band, bearers and a retinue of lesser masquerades. Traditionally performed at the burial of the most illustrious titled men and at great festivals, it appears so rarely that its emergence is a civic event — the 'roof' under which lesser masquerades may perform, reading law, wealth and cosmology in its stacked imagery. Dancers inside animate the giant while akunechenyi music plays, and Ndi-Ijele associations have carried the form to national festivals as an emblem of Igbo monumental performance art.",
